| ---: | ---: | ---: | ---: | ---: | ---: | ---: |
| 1 | 2.142857 | 0.489796 | 0.081633 | 0.571429 | 2 | 3 |


| observation | response | fitted | residual | leverage | loo_residual |
| ---: | ---: | ---: | ---: | ---: | ---: |
| 1 | 0 | 0.285714 | -0.285714 | 0.857143 | -2 |
| 2 | 1 | 0.428571 | 0.571429 | 0.428571 | 1 |
| 3 | 0 | 0.285714 | -0.285714 | 0.857143 | -2 |

\image(figures/pspline_three_points.svg)
// result_end
\caption(Exact three-point penalised least squares. The basis is fixed across the three curves; lambda alone changes their curvature. Tables describe the lambda-one fit.)
\alttext(Left: three triangular basis functions form an identity design at zero, one and two. Right: the unpenalised triangle passes through the observations, lambda one flattens it, and lambda 100 nearly reaches the constant one-third least-squares line.)

At lambda one, the leverages are \((6/7,3/7,6/7)\) and the delete-one residuals
are \((-2,1,-2)\). Their squared mean is 3, while GCV is 2. Deletion keeps the
original basis, including both endpoint knots: the two retained observations determine
a line, and a deleted endpoint requires linear continuation of that constrained fit.
The facade's separate prediction rule clamps *outside* the original full-data domain.

>> A cubic basis: first differences versus second differences

Use seven points from \(y=2-x\) on \([0,3]\) and six cubic basis functions.
Uniform spacing is one, with knots extended by three on each side. The Greville
coordinates, averages of the three inner knots for each function, are
\((-1,0,1,2,3,4)\). Polynomial reproduction gives coefficients
\((3,2,1,0,-1,-2)\). Their first differences are all \(-1\), but their second
differences vanish:

$$
D_1\beta=(-1,-1,-1,-1,-1)^\top,\quad \|D_1\beta\|^2=5,
\qquad D_2\beta=0.
$$
\alttext(The line's six cubic coefficients descend by one at every step, so the first-difference roughness is five and the second-difference roughness is zero.)

The second-order penalty preserves the line for every identifiable multiplier.
The first-order penalty instead pulls its slope towards zero, approaching the response
mean 0.5 as lambda grows. The coefficient plot makes the constraint visible.

```{cpp, results="markdown", file="figures/pspline_nullspace.svg", flags="-std=c++23 -O2 $(pkg-config --cflags --libs datamunge) -Wl,-rpath,$(pkg-config --variable=libdir datamunge)"}
#include <dm/dm.hpp>
int main() {
  const dm::Vector<> x=dm::seql(0,3,7), y=2-x, grid=dm::seql(0,3,201), index=dm::seql(0,5,6);
  const dm::PSR first(x,y,6,100.0,3,1), second(x,y,6,100.0,3,2);
  auto coefficients=dm::graphics::RPlot::create(), curves=dm::graphics::RPlot::create();
  coefficients.lines(index.values(),second.coefficients().values(),"Order 2",{37,99,235},3)
    .points(index.values(),second.coefficients().values(),"",{37,99,235},4)
    .lines(index.values(),first.coefficients().values(),"Order 1",{220,90,35},3)
    .title("A line has affine coefficients").x_label("Basis index j").y_label("Coefficient beta_j").y_limits(-2.4,4.5);
  curves.points(x.values(),y.values(),"Observed line",{70,70,70},4)
    .lines(grid.values(),second.predict(grid).values(),"Order 2",{37,99,235},3)
    .lines(grid.values(),first.predict(grid).values(),"Order 1",{220,90,35},3)
    .title("Same cubic basis, lambda = 100").x_label("x").y_label("Response").y_limits(-1.3,3.5);
  auto layout=dm::graphics::RLayout::create(1,2); layout.add(coefficients).add(curves);
  std::filesystem::create_directories("build");
  layout.size(1100,490).fit_panels().save_svg("build/pspline_nullspace.svg");
  std::print("{}\n",first.summary().concat_rows(second.summary())
    .select({"order","lambda","edf","rss","roughness"}).to_md(9));
}
```
