\image(figures/pspline_exact_path.svg)
// result_end
\caption(A six-decade sweep checks the exact shrinkage law. The path simultaneously verifies decreasing roughness, increasing RSS and constant positive-lambda CV/GCV.)
\alttext(Left: computed effective degrees of freedom lie on the exact curve, descending from almost three to almost two as lambda grows. Right: roughness drops from almost four to zero, while RSS rises from zero towards two thirds.)

>> Known truth: one global multiplier for an oscillating signal

Retain the original example's mean \(f(x)=\sin(20/(x+0.25))\), whose oscillations
are faster near zero. It is an oscillating test function, rather than the usual
wavelet Doppler benchmark. Here 401 equally spaced inputs have independent seeded
normal noise with standard deviation 0.3. We compare zero penalty, GCV and a large
fixed multiplier on exactly the same 50-function extended cubic basis.

The right panel uses the whole score path, not training RSS as a selection rule.
Known truth allows a separate grid error calculation: GCV estimates predictive
performance from data and need not minimise error to this particular true curve.
A single global penalty can smooth away fast local oscillations before suppressing
all noise in the slow region.

```{cpp, results="markdown", file="figures/pspline_oscillation.svg", flags="-std=c++23 -O2 $(pkg-config --cflags --libs datamunge) -Wl,-rpath,$(pkg-config --variable=libdir datamunge)"}
#include <dm/dm.hpp>
int main() {
  dm::set_seed(20261028);
  const dm::Vector<> x=dm::seql(0,1,401), y=dm::sin(20/(x+.25))+dm::rnorm(0,.3,401), grid=dm::seql(0,1,801);
  const dm::PSR raw(x,y,50,0.0), chosen(x,y,50), heavy(x,y,50,1000.0);
  const dm::Vector<> logs=dm::seql(-5,4,37);
  const auto path=dm::PSR::smoothing_path(x,y,50,dm::exp(logs*std::log(10.0)));
  auto curves=dm::graphics::RPlot::create(), scores=dm::graphics::RPlot::create();
  curves.points(x.values(),y.values(),"Samples",{170,170,170,.45},2)
    .lines(grid.values(),raw.predict(grid).values(),"lambda = 0",{25,145,105})
    .lines(grid.values(),heavy.predict(grid).values(),"lambda = 1000",{220,90,35})
    .lines(grid.values(),dm::sin(20/(grid+.25)).values(),"True mean",{65,65,65},2)
    .lines(grid.values(),chosen.predict(grid).values(),"GCV fit",{37,99,235},2)
    .title("Fast oscillations are harder to preserve").x_label("x").y_label("Response").y_limits(-2.3,3.5);
  scores.lines(logs.values(),path.numeric_column("gcv").values(),"GCV",{37,99,235},3)
    .lines(logs.values(),path.numeric_column("loo_cv").values(),"Delete-one CV",{220,90,35},2)
    .abline_v(std::log10(chosen.lambda()),{70,70,70})
    .title("Predictive scores on the same basis").x_label("log10(lambda)").y_label("Squared response");
  auto layout=dm::graphics::RLayout::create(1,2); layout.add(curves).add(scores);
  std::filesystem::create_directories("build");
  layout.size(1100,510).fit_panels().save_svg("build/pspline_oscillation.svg");
  auto table=raw.summary().concat_rows(chosen.summary()).concat_rows(heavy.summary());
  std::vector<double> rmse;
  for (const auto* fit : {&raw,&chosen,&heavy}) rmse.push_back(std::sqrt(dm::mean(dm::pow(fit->predict(grid)-dm::sin(20/(grid+.25)),2))));
  table.add_column("mean_grid_rmse",rmse);
  std::print("{}\n",table.select({"lambda","edf","rss","roughness","gcv","loo_cv","mean_grid_rmse"}).to_md(6));
}
```
// result_begin: markdown
| lambda | edf | rss | roughness | gcv | loo_cv | mean_grid_rmse |
| ---: | ---: | ---: | ---: | ---: | ---: | ---: |
| 0.000000 | 50.000000 | 61.141569 | 831.890961 | 0.199006 | 0.201738 | 0.279934 |
| 0.001563 | 48.978355 | 61.344891 | 523.042362 | 0.198511 | 0.199525 | 0.279070 |
| 1000.000000 | 6.014085 | 187.679484 | 0.009494 | 0.482390 | 0.482227 | 0.623732 |

\image(figures/pspline_oscillation.svg)
// result_end
\caption(The original oscillating signal revisited with a controlled basis comparison. Table rows are zero penalty, GCV selection and lambda 1000. Mean-grid RMSE measures recovery of the noiseless curve, not prediction of a new noisy response.)
\alttext(Left: noisy observations and the true oscillating mean are compared with unpenalised, GCV and heavily penalised fits. The heavy penalty loses oscillations near zero. Right: GCV and delete-one CV increase as the multiplier becomes too large; a vertical line marks the GCV-selected value.)

GCV selects lambda 0.001563 and keeps 48.98 effective degrees of freedom, almost the
full basis. Its mean-grid RMSE is 0.2791, versus 0.2799 without a penalty and 0.6237
under heavy smoothing. Crucially, even the unpenalised fit misses the fastest oscillations
near zero: this 50-function basis lacks resolution there. A smaller penalty cannot repair
that approximation error. A richer basis is needed before treating this as solely a
smoothing-selection problem; a global penalty can then still face unequal local complexity.

> A dataset example: Old Faithful

>> Data, units and the modelling question

`dm::datasets::faithful()` contains 272 rows and two complete numeric columns.
We use eruption duration in minutes as \(x\), and waiting time to the next eruption
in minutes as \(y\). R's [dataset documentation|https://stat.ethz.ch/R-manual/R-devel/library/datasets/html/faithful.html]
notes that eruption times are heavily rounded. Repeated durations are legitimate
replicate inputs, not duplicate observations to remove. The two clusters leave
relatively little information about the middle of the curve.

```{cpp, results="markdown", flags="-std=c++23 -O2 $(pkg-config --cflags --libs datamunge) -Wl,-rpath,$(pkg-config --variable=libdir datamunge)"}
#include <dm/dm.hpp>
int main() {
  const auto data=dm::datasets::faithful();
  std::print("{} eruptions, {} columns.\n\n{}\n",data.nrows(),data.ncols(),data
    | dm::pivot_longer({"eruptions","waiting"},"variable","minutes") | dm::group_by("variable")
    | dm::summarise(dm::agg::n("n"),dm::agg::mean("minutes","mean"),dm::agg::sd("minutes","sd"),
                    dm::agg::min("minutes","min"),dm::agg::max("minutes","max")) | dm::to_md(3));
}
```
// result_begin: markdown
272 eruptions, 2 columns.

| variable | n | mean | sd | min | max |
| :--- | ---: | ---: | ---: | ---: | ---: |
| eruptions | 272 | 3.488 | 1.141 | 1.6 | 5.1 |
| waiting | 272 | 70.897 | 13.595 | 43.0 | 96.0 |
// result_end
\caption(Old Faithful variable summaries, both measured in minutes. All 272 paired observations are retained; no standardisation or missing-value deletion is needed.)

>> Fitting a rich basis and inspecting the path

Start with 25 cubic basis functions and second differences. We compare a small
fixed multiplier with automatic GCV selection and a very large multiplier approaching
the least-squares line. The data-loss convention and knot spacing matter: lambda
itself is not comparable across different basis sizes or different packages.
The high-penalty curve is a useful reference, not a physically constrained geyser model.

```{cpp, results="markdown", file="figures/pspline_faithful.svg", flags="-std=c++23 -O2 $(pkg-config --cflags --libs datamunge) -Wl,-rpath,$(pkg-config --variable=libdir datamunge)"}
#include <dm/dm.hpp>
int main() {
  const auto data=dm::datasets::faithful();
  const dm::Vector<> x=data.numeric_column("eruptions").values(), y=data.numeric_column("waiting").values();
  const dm::PSR light(x,y,25,.001), chosen(x,y,25), heavy(x,y,25,1e6);
  const auto [lo,hi]=chosen.domain(); const auto grid=dm::seql(lo,hi,301), logs=dm::seql(-4,6,41);
  const auto path=dm::PSR::smoothing_path(x,y,25,dm::exp(logs*std::log(10.0)));
  auto curves=dm::graphics::RPlot::create(), scores=dm::graphics::RPlot::create();
  curves.points(x.values(),y.values(),"Eruptions",{110,110,110,.45},3)
    .lines(grid.values(),light.predict(grid).values(),"lambda = .001",{25,145,105})
    .lines(grid.values(),heavy.predict(grid).values(),"Near-line fit",{220,90,35},2)
    .lines(grid.values(),chosen.predict(grid).values(),"GCV fit",{37,99,235},3)
    .title("Next wait versus eruption duration").x_label("Eruption duration (min)").y_label("Next waiting time (min)").y_limits(35,125);
  scores.lines(logs.values(),path.numeric_column("gcv").values(),"GCV",{37,99,235},3)
    .lines(logs.values(),path.numeric_column("loo_cv").values(),"Delete-one CV",{220,90,35},2)
    .abline_v(std::log10(chosen.lambda()),{70,70,70})
    .title("Inspect the score, not only the minimum").x_label("log10(lambda)").y_label("Squared minutes");
  auto layout=dm::graphics::RLayout::create(1,2); layout.add(curves).add(scores);
  std::filesystem::create_directories("build");
  layout.size(1100,510).fit_panels().save_svg("build/pspline_faithful.svg");
  std::print("{}\n",light.summary().concat_rows(chosen.summary()).concat_rows(heavy.summary())
    .select({"lambda","edf","rss","roughness","r_squared","gcv","loo_cv"}).to_md(6));
}
```
// result_begin: markdown
| lambda | edf | rss | roughness | r_squared | gcv | loo_cv |
| ---: | ---: | ---: | ---: | ---: | ---: | ---: |
| 0.001000 | 24.036923 | 7740.40909 | 49723.961321 | 0.845461 | 34.241973 | 44.909952 |
| 78.048067 | 5.267982 | 8415.96091 | 1.290617 | 0.831974 | 32.175278 | 32.385646 |
| 1000000.000000 | 2.004472 | 9438.84242 | 0.000002 | 0.811551 | 35.218795 | 35.197342 |

\image(figures/pspline_faithful.svg)
// result_end
\caption(A 25-function fit and its multiplier path. Table rows are light smoothing, the GCV choice and a nearly linear fit. The vertical line marks automatic selection; the path shows its surrounding sensitivity.)
\alttext(Left: the two Old Faithful clusters are overlaid with lightly smoothed, GCV and nearly linear curves. Right: GCV and exact fixed-lambda delete-one scores are plotted over ten decades of smoothing.)

The selected multiplier is 78.048, with edf 5.268 and in-sample R squared 0.8320.
GCV is 32.175 square minutes; exact fixed-lambda CV is 32.386, or an RMSE of
5.69 minutes. The light fit has edf 24.04: its CV score of 44.91 is substantially
higher than its GCV score of 34.24, showing the effect of uneven leverage in this
irregularly populated design. The near-line fit has edf 2.004 and CV 35.20.

>> Residuals, leverage and basis-size sensitivity

A useful mean curve does not account for every source of variation. Plot residuals
in the original response units and inspect leverage where durations are unusual.
A large corrected deletion residual can reflect either a large raw residual or a
large leverage; it is not automatically a data error.

We also retune at 12, 25 and 50 basis functions. A rich basis gives the penalty room
to work, but finite-sample fits and GCV scores need not be invariant to its size.
The table compares the resulting effective complexity, not just the nominal coefficient
count. The deletion scores are internal diagnostics after selection; they do not
constitute an independent test set or validation for future eruption sequences.

```{cpp, results="markdown", file="figures/pspline_faithful_diagnostics.svg", flags="-std=c++23 -O2 $(pkg-config --cflags --libs datamunge) -Wl,-rpath,$(pkg-config --variable=libdir datamunge)"}
#include <dm/dm.hpp>
int main() {
  const auto data=dm::datasets::faithful();
  const dm::Vector<> x=data.numeric_column("eruptions").values(), y=data.numeric_column("waiting").values();
  const dm::PSR fit(x,y,25); const auto details=fit.observation_details();
  auto residuals=dm::graphics::RPlot::create(), leverage=dm::graphics::RPlot::create();
  residuals.points(details.numeric_column("fitted").values(),details.numeric_column("residual").values(),"",{37,99,235,.5},3)
    .abline_h(0,{70,70,70}).title("Residual variation remains").x_label("Fitted next wait (min)").y_label("Observed - fitted (min)");
  leverage.points(x.values(),details.numeric_column("leverage").values(),"",{220,90,35,.6},3)
    .abline_h(fit.effective_df()/x.size(),{70,70,70})
    .title("Leverage depends on local data support").x_label("Eruption duration (min)").y_label("Leverage h_ii");
  auto layout=dm::graphics::RLayout::create(1,2); layout.add(residuals).add(leverage);
  std::filesystem::create_directories("build");
  layout.size(1100,490).fit_panels().save_svg("build/pspline_faithful_diagnostics.svg");
  auto sensitivity=dm::PSR(x,y,12).summary();
  for (std::size_t k : {25u,50u}) sensitivity=sensitivity.concat_rows(dm::PSR(x,y,k).summary());
  std::print("{}\n",sensitivity.select({"basis","lambda","edf","rss","gcv","loo_cv"}).to_md(6));
}
```
// result_begin: markdown
| basis | lambda | edf | rss | gcv | loo_cv |
| ---: | ---: | ---: | ---: | ---: | ---: |
| 12 | 4.154346 | 5.124265 | 8416.21340 | 32.141598 | 32.353339 |
| 25 | 78.048067 | 5.267982 | 8415.96091 | 32.175278 | 32.385646 |
| 50 | 789.294958 | 5.327497 | 8413.73248 | 32.181118 | 32.392013 |

\image(figures/pspline_faithful_diagnostics.svg)
// result_end
\caption(Residuals and leverage for the 25-function GCV fit, followed by sensitivity to retuned basis size. The leverage reference is edf/n; it is a mean, not an outlier threshold.)
\alttext(Left: observed minus fitted waiting times are plotted against fitted waiting times with a zero reference. Right: leverage is plotted against eruption duration, with its mean marked by a horizontal line.)

Across 12, 25 and 50 basis functions, automatic selection gives edf 5.12, 5.27 and
5.33, while GCV remains between 32.14 and 32.19 square minutes. The conclusion is
stable at this level of resolution, although the tuning multipliers themselves change.
Residuals still span roughly minus 14 to plus 14 minutes, and leverage rises at the
ends and in the sparsely sampled middle. Smoothing removes neither that variation
nor the need to examine the sampling process.

> Conclusion

The three-point system isolates the mechanism: two unpenalised line modes and one
shrinking curvature mode. Its exact path verifies the equations and also exposes a
case where neither GCV nor delete-one CV can choose a unique positive multiplier.
The cubic example shows that penalty order and boundary convention determine what
survives smoothing. They deserve as much attention as the spline degree.

On known truth, stronger smoothing trades lower coefficient roughness for higher
training error and can erase fast oscillations. On Old Faithful, the fitted conditional
mean is useful for description, while residuals, sparse regions and basis sensitivity
limit its interpretation. GCV supplies a data-driven choice within a specified basis;
it does not supply uncertainty bands, enforce monotonicity, remove serial dependence,
or validate extrapolation. `PSR` clamps predictions outside its fitted domain.

> References

- P. H. C. Eilers and B. D. Marx (1996). *Flexible smoothing with B-splines and penalties.* Statistical Science 11(2), 89–102 (discussion and rejoinder to 121). [Original article|https://doi.org/10.1214/ss/1038425655].
- G. H. Golub, M. Heath and G. Wahba (1979). *Generalized cross-validation as a method for choosing a good ridge parameter.* Technometrics 21(2), 215–223. [Original article|https://doi.org/10.1080/00401706.1979.10489751].
- R Core Team. [splineDesign|https://stat.ethz.ch/R-manual/R-devel/library/splines/html/splineDesign.html], for an independent evaluation of an identical knot sequence, and [Old Faithful data|https://stat.ethz.ch/R-manual/R-devel/library/datasets/html/faithful.html], for variable meanings and rounding caveats.
